AIM:
Study of Topology - Benchmark LAN Trainer Kit
To study the various network topologies - bus, star, ring, mesh, tree and hybrid- and to
physically construct and observe them using the Benchmark LAN Trainer Kit.

SOFTWARE REQUIRED:
• Benchmark LAN Trainer Kit with its node modules, central hub/switch module and
patch cords.
• Set of connecting cables (RJ-45 patch cords or the kit's supplied connectors).
• Kit power supply and the accompanying observation/indicator panel.

THEORY:
Network topology is the physical or logical arrangement in which nodes (computers,
terminals or the kit's node modules) are interconnected. The topology determines how data
flows, how much cabling is needed, how the network behaves when a link or node fails, and
how easy it is to expand. A LAN trainer kit provides node modules and a central connecting
module so that each topology can be wired up by hand and its behaviour — signal path, fault
effect, expandability — directly observed on the kit's indicator panel.

PROCEDURE:
1. Place the LAN Trainer Kit on the bench and connect its power supply. Switch it on
and confirm the indicator lamps/LEDs are functioning.
2. Identify the components: the individual node modules, the central hub/switch module,
the backbone connector, and the supplied patch cords.
Bus topology
3. Connect all node modules along the single common backbone line provided on the
kit, fitting the terminators at both ends of the bus.
4. Send data from one node and observe on the indicator panel that the signal travels
along the shared backbone and is seen by all nodes; only the addressed node accepts
it.
5. Disconnect the backbone at a mid-point and note that communication beyond the
break stops — demonstrating the bus's single point of failure.
Star topology
6. Connect each node module by a separate patch cord to a port on the central
hub/switch module.
7. Transmit from one node and observe that data passes through the central module to
reach the destination node.
8. Disconnect one node's cable and confirm the other nodes continue to communicate;
then power off the central module and note that the whole network stops — showing
the star's dependence on the central device.
Ring topology
9. Wire the node modules in a closed loop — the output of each node to the input of the
next — until the last node connects back to the first.
10. Send data and observe it circulating from node to node around the ring until it reaches
the intended destination.
